HST Office: Word mail-merge documents keep their fields. The DOCX import dropped every switch of a
MERGEFIELD (\# "#,##0", \@ "dd/MM/yyyy", \* Upper, \b, \f) and turned an IF that compares a nested
MERGEFIELD into a conditional text whose condition had lost the merge field ("FORMULA Nam"); saving
the document again wrote those broken instructions, so Word merged wrong results as well.

Such fields are now database fields whose column name is the Word instruction, nested fields written
{ ... }: "MERGEFIELD Lương \# "#,##0"", "IF { MERGEFIELD Giới_tính } = "Nam" "Ông" "Bà"". The import
keeps each field's instruction with its nested fields while it reads it (one member of FieldContext).
The HST mail merge (hst-vietnamese-tools, mail_merge_word_*.py) gives the staging copy of the
recipients one column per instruction, computed with Word's rules, so the engine merges Word's
result. The DOCX export writes such a column back as the instruction with real nested fields; DOC
and RTF, which cannot nest them here, write the shown text. A MERGEFIELD without switches stays a
field named by its column, as before.

When the HST mail merge names the recipient list in the document's InteropGrabBag
"HSTMailMergeSource" (Path, Sheet), w:mailMerge is written the way Word links an Excel sheet or a
text file (w:dataSource relationship, OLE DB connect string, query), so Word reconnects to the list;
before, it wrote the internal data source name, which Word cannot open.

The logic is in new HST files (sw/source/writerfilter/hst/hstwordmergeimport.*,
sw/source/filter/ww8/hst/hstwordmergeexport.*); the LibreOffice files only call them.

Test: CppunitTest_sw_hst_mailmerge_word (import, DOCX round trip with nested fields and the Word link
of the list, mail merge of instruction-named columns from a Calc staging copy).
